  {
   "user": "O_oNevermore",
   "message": "---\n\nthere is this one tribe where they change their name semi-frequently (e.g. different phases of life, or when witnessing something you couldn't explain, or your \"old name rots\")\n\nthey stop even responding to the old name and deny having had those experiences \"are you talking about that other guy? I'm not him\" \n\neven after decades of living with them dan Everett couldn't get them to admit having had the experiences of their previous identities, their recounting becomes vague and they say that was someone else\n\nimagine if we teach them to stop doing that, e.g. by introducing the concept of the soul\n\nfrom the outside they would just keep their name, but from the inside, and as a tribe or culture, would their experience of the world change? superficially yes ofc, but more deeply (e.g. now they have to cope with experiences from different identities that they would otherwise have been able to dissociate)? would they weave this phenomenological difference into their myths?\n\nwe take it for granted that the concept of a self exists everywhere, but it had to be invented, it had to evolve, and cultures changed as a consequence",
   "timestamp": "2026-08-30T09:49:23.264000+00:00",
